// kernel/netview.fi -- round NETVIEW: ONE NETWORK PER PROCESS.
//
// A great many programs refuse to work without a network although they
// would work perfectly well without one. They do not need the network
// for what the person in front of the screen asked for; they need it to
// fetch an advertisement, to report a start, to check a licence. The
// answer this system gives is not "block them" -- a blocked program
// shows an error box and stops. The answer is: GIVE THE PROGRAM A
// NETWORK THAT LOOKS NORMAL AND LEADS NOWHERE.
//
// WHY THIS IS CHEAP HERE AND EXPENSIVE EVERYWHERE ELSE. Osum carries its
// own TCP/IP stack (`kernel/inet.fi` over the stack of round K3) and its
// own socket layer (`kernel/sys.fi`). Every octet a process wants to put
// on the wire passes through eleven functions that this repository
// wrote. So a per-process network is a FIELD IN THE TASK RECORD and a
// question asked at the door -- not a virtual device, not a namespace,
// not a packet filter. `docs/NETVIEW.md` holds that comparison against
// Android, Linux and Windows; it is not repeated here.
//
// THE FOUR VIEWS:
//
//   real      the wire, exactly as before. The default, and what every
//             process that existed before this round still gets.
//   filtered  a list of destinations is reachable for real; everything
//             else is answered as in `faked`.
//   faked     there is an address, a gateway, a name server and a
//             connection -- and nothing behind them. Described below.
//   none      no network. Every attempt fails at once with
//             -ENETUNREACH, the way a machine with the cable pulled
//             fails.
//
// The numbers are ordered BY HOW MUCH THEY ALLOW, real (0) lowest and
// none (3) highest, and one rule is written on that order: a process may
// always RAISE its own number and never lower it (`may_set`). That is
// what lets `/bin/netview faked <program>` work with no privilege at
// all, and what stops the faked program from handing itself the wire
// back -- or handing it to a sibling.
//
// HOW A FAKED CONNECTION ENDS, and this is the whole design decision of
// the round. Three answers were possible and two of them are wrong:
//
//   REFUSE (RST at once).  Honest, fast -- and useless. A program that
//   sees ECONNREFUSED knows it is offline and shows the error box this
//   round exists to avoid.
//
//   SWALLOW (accept the SYN, never answer).  This is the worst of the
//   three and it is what a naive "drop the packets" firewall does. The
//   program hangs in `connect` until ITS timeout runs out -- thirty
//   seconds, sixty, sometimes forever. Offline would have been better.
//
//   ACCEPT AND END IT (what is built here).  The connection succeeds
//   IMMEDIATELY, in the same system call, with no wire and no wait. What
//   the program writes is taken and dropped. What it reads is a defined,
//   short answer and then an ordinary end of stream. The program's own
//   error path for "the server said nothing useful" runs, which is the
//   path it is tested on -- and it runs in microseconds instead of
//   minutes.
//
// AND ONE ANSWER IS NOT EMPTY. Very many programs decide whether they
// are online by fetching one URL and looking at the status. Android asks
// for a 204, Windows for a short text file, GNOME for a fixed body. So
// if what the program wrote LOOKS LIKE AN HTTP REQUEST, what it reads
// back is a well-formed `HTTP/1.1 204 No Content` with no body. The
// reachability check SUCCEEDS, the download stays EMPTY. That is
// precisely the point of the round.
//
// NAMES RESOLVE. A program that fails at `getaddrinfo` never reaches the
// interesting code. So a datagram to port 53 is answered here, out of
// the question that was asked, with an A record pointing at
// 198.51.100.80. Every address this file invents comes out of TEST-NET-2
// (RFC 5737), which is reserved for documentation and is guaranteed by
// the registry never to be routed anywhere. An answer this file makes up
// therefore cannot collide with a machine that exists.
//
// NOT ONE OCTET LEAVES. A faked socket never calls a function of
// `inet.fi` or `stack.fi`, and those are the only two things in this
// kernel that can hand a frame to `virtio.tx_frame`. The proof is not
// this sentence: `tools/netview/run.sh` runs the same image twice, once
// `real` and once `faked`, and reads `nic: tx_o=` out of the kernel's
// own report. Real: some thousands. Faked: zero.
//
// WHERE THE STATE LIVES. The view and the allow list live in the TASK
// RECORD (`sched.T_NETV`, `sched.T_NETOK`) and nowhere else -- there is
// no second table to forget. What lives here, in `pci.K2_SCALARS`, is
// something different and it is per SOCKET, not per process: whether
// THIS connection was faked, what it was aimed at, and how far the
// canned answer has been handed out. A process can hold four sockets and
// they need four read positions; that cannot live in the process. The
// view itself is read out of the task on every single call and never
// copied here.
//
// WHAT THIS FILE DOES NOT DO, said plainly:
//
//   * TLS. A faked answer to a connection on port 443 is impossible --
//     the client would have to be able to verify a certificate for a
//     name, and forging one means being a certificate authority the
//     client trusts. A faked TLS connection therefore gets an immediate,
//     clean end of stream, and the program sees "the server hung up",
//     not "no network". That is the best available answer and it is a
//     limit, not a feature.
//   * A LISTENING socket in `faked` or `none` binds and listens without
//     ever touching the stack, and `accept` says -EAGAIN at once. Nobody
//     will ever connect to it, because there is no wire under it.
//   * ONE view per process, not one per socket. A program that wants the
//     real network for one connection and a faked one for another is
//     asking for a policy engine; this is a field.

profile kernel

import kstate
import sched
import pci
import errno
import serial
import inet
import virtio

export {
    V_REAL, V_FILTERED, V_FAKED, V_NONE, V_LAST,
    view_of, set_view, may_set, inherit, restrictive,
    allow_count, allow_at, allow_add, allow_clear, allowed,
    D_REAL, D_DENY, D_FAKE, decide,
    reset, release, is_fake, marked,
    open_fake, send_fake, recv_fake, sendto_fake, recvfrom_fake,
    peer_of,
    FAKE_IP, FAKE_MASK, FAKE_GW, FAKE_DNS, FAKE_SINK,
    FAKE_MAC0, FAKE_MAC1,
    sa_scratch, peek_buf, PEEK_MAX,
    dropped, opened, denials, replies, report
}

// ------------------------------------------------------------ the views
//
// Ordered by how much they allow. `may_set` depends on that order.

const V_REAL: u64 = 0
const V_FILTERED: u64 = 1
const V_FAKED: u64 = 2
const V_NONE: u64 = 3
const V_LAST: u64 = 3

// What `decide` answers.
const D_REAL: u64 = 0 // let it through to the stack
const D_DENY: u64 = 1 // -ENETUNREACH, at once
const D_FAKE: u64 = 2 // answer it here

// ------------------------------------------- the invented network
//
// TEST-NET-2, 198.51.100.0/24 (RFC 5737). Reserved for documentation,
// never routed. An address made up in this file can therefore not be an
// address that exists.
const FAKE_IP: u64 = 0xC6336402 // 198.51.100.2
const FAKE_MASK: u64 = 0xFFFFFF00 // /24
const FAKE_GW: u64 = 0xC6336401 // 198.51.100.1
const FAKE_DNS: u64 = 0xC6336435 // 198.51.100.53
const FAKE_SINK: u64 = 0xC6336450 // 198.51.100.80 -- what names resolve to

// A locally administered hardware address (bit 1 of the first octet).
// The real one of the card is not handed to a faked process: it is a
// number that identifies the machine, and this round is about not
// leaking such numbers.
const FAKE_MAC0: u64 = 0x02005645 // 02:00:56:45
const FAKE_MAC1: u64 = 0x0002 // :00:02

// --------------------------------------------- the place in `kdata`
//
// `pci.K2_SCALARS` is 0x12000..0x14000. Round K2 uses up to 0x2A0,
// `hw.fi` and `netsvc.fi` up to 0x3F8, `inet.fi` puts its socket table
// at 0x400, its address cache at 0x600 and its ICMP queue at 0x700..
// 0xB00. This round takes 0xB00 upwards and stays under 0x1400.

const NV_SOCK: u64 = 0xB00 // one record per socket
const NV_BYTES: u64 = 64
const NV_BUF: u64 = 0x1000 // the canned answer of each socket
const NV_BUFB: u64 = 256

// Two scratch areas, and they exist for a reason that is older than
// this round: `inet.saddr` and `inet.bounce` are FRAMES THE CARD'S
// DRIVER ALLOCATES. On a machine with no network card there is no card,
// no `inet.init`, and both of them are zero -- and a faked network is
// exactly the thing that has to work on such a machine, because that is
// the machine the person is sitting at when the aeroplane has no wifi.
// So the faked paths use these, which are part of `kdata` and therefore
// exist from the first instruction.
const NV_SA: u64 = 0x1400 // two sockaddr_in, in and out
const NV_PEEK: u64 = 0x1480 // what a faked `send` is looked at through
const PEEK_MAX: u64 = 256

const NV_DROPPED: u64 = 0xF00 // octets a faked socket swallowed
const NV_OPENED: u64 = 0xF08 // faked connections opened
const NV_DENIED: u64 = 0xF10 // attempts answered -ENETUNREACH
const NV_REPLIES: u64 = 0xF18 // canned answers built

// The fields of one faked socket.
const F_USE: u64 = 0 // 0 = an ordinary socket, 1 = a faked one
const F_KIND: u64 = 8 // inet.SK_TCP / SK_UDP / SK_ICMP
const F_ADDR: u64 = 16 // where the program thinks it is talking
const F_PORT: u64 = 24
const F_POS: u64 = 32 // how much of the answer was handed out
const F_LEN: u64 = 40 // how long the answer is
const F_SENT: u64 = 48 // octets this socket swallowed
const F_SEEN: u64 = 56 // 1 = the first block was already looked at

fn sc(off: u64) -> u64 {
    return pci.K2_SCALARS + off
}

fn at(i: u64) -> u64 {
    return sc(NV_SOCK + i * NV_BYTES)
}

fn buf(i: u64) -> u64 {
    return sc(NV_BUF + i * NV_BUFB)
}

fn sa_scratch(state: u64) -> u64 {
    return state + sc(NV_SA)
}

fn peek_buf(state: u64) -> u64 {
    return state + sc(NV_PEEK)
}

// ------------------------------------------------------- the counters

fn dropped(state: u64) -> u64 {
    return kstate.get(state, sc(NV_DROPPED))
}

fn opened(state: u64) -> u64 {
    return kstate.get(state, sc(NV_OPENED))
}

fn denials(state: u64) -> u64 {
    return kstate.get(state, sc(NV_DENIED))
}

fn replies(state: u64) -> u64 {
    return kstate.get(state, sc(NV_REPLIES))
}

// ------------------------------------------------- the view of a task
//
// ONE PLACE. `sched.T_NETV` in the task record, read on every call, and
// there is no copy of it anywhere -- not in the socket, not in a table
// beside the task table. A view that is changed while a program runs is
// therefore in force for the next call and not "the next time it starts".

fn view_of(state: u64, t: u64) -> u64 {
    let v: u64 = sched.tget(state, t, sched.T_NETV)
    if v > V_LAST {
        return V_REAL
    }
    return v
}

// Is `to` at least as restrictive as `from`?
fn restrictive(from: u64, to: u64) -> bool {
    return to >= from
}

// May `who` set the view of `whom` to `want`? The rule of the round:
//
//   * TIGHTENING YOUR OWN VIEW is always allowed and needs no
//     privilege. This is the one that matters: it is how a launcher
//     drops its own network between `fork` and `execve`, and how
//     `/bin/netview faked <program>` works for an ordinary user.
//   * EVERYTHING ELSE needs euid 0 -- loosening your own view, and
//     touching another process at all, in either direction.
//
// The second half is not caution, it is the whole guarantee. Were a
// process allowed to loosen its own view, the faked program would set
// itself back to `real` in one call. Were it allowed to touch a process
// with the same user, it would set a SIBLING to `real` and have the
// sibling fetch. Both are escapes, and both are closed by the same line.
fn may_set(state: u64, who: u64, whom: u64, want: u64) -> bool {
    if want > V_LAST {
        return false
    }
    if sched.tget(state, who, sched.T_EUID) == 0 {
        return true
    }
    if who != whom {
        return false
    }
    return restrictive(view_of(state, who), want)
}

// Sets it. The caller has already asked `may_set`; this function is the
// write and nothing else, so that there is exactly one of each.
fn set_view(state: u64, t: u64, v: u64) {
    if v > V_LAST {
        return
    }
    sched.tset(state, t, sched.T_NETV, v)
}

// ---------------------------------------------------- the allow list
//
// Six entries in the task record, one word each, 0 = empty:
//
//   bits 63..32  the address
//   bits 31..16  the port, 0 = any port
//   bits 15..8   the prefix length, 0..32
//   bit 0        in use
//
// Six and not sixty: this is the list of "the one service this program
// really needs", and a list long enough to be a firewall rule set would
// be a firewall, in the wrong place, in a task record.

fn allow_at(state: u64, t: u64, i: u64) -> u64 {
    if i >= sched.NETOK_MAX {
        return 0
    }
    return sched.tget(state, t, sched.T_NETOK + i * 8)
}

fn allow_count(state: u64, t: u64) -> u64 {
    var n: u64 = 0
    var i: u64 = 0
    while i < sched.NETOK_MAX {
        if (allow_at(state, t, i) & 1) != 0 {
            n = n + 1
        }
        i = i + 1
    }
    return n
}

fn allow_clear(state: u64, t: u64) {
    var i: u64 = 0
    while i < sched.NETOK_MAX {
        sched.tset(state, t, sched.T_NETOK + i * 8, 0)
        i = i + 1
    }
}

fn allow_add(state: u64, t: u64, addr: u64, port: u64,
    prefix: u64) -> bool {
    if prefix > 32 || port > 65535 {
        return false
    }
    var i: u64 = 0
    while i < sched.NETOK_MAX {
        if (allow_at(state, t, i) & 1) == 0 {
            sched.tset(state, t, sched.T_NETOK + i * 8,
                ((addr & 0xFFFFFFFF) << 32) | ((port & 0xFFFF) << 16)
                | ((prefix & 0xFF) << 8) | 1)
            return true
        }
        i = i + 1
    }
    return false
}

// The mask of a prefix length. Written out rather than shifted blindly:
// `0xFFFFFFFF << 32` is not a mask of /0, it is a word of zeros in the
// low half, and a /0 entry means EVERY address.
fn mask_of(prefix: u64) -> u64 {
    if prefix == 0 {
        return 0
    }
    if prefix >= 32 {
        return 0xFFFFFFFF
    }
    return (0xFFFFFFFF << (32 - prefix)) & 0xFFFFFFFF
}

fn allowed(state: u64, t: u64, addr: u64, port: u64) -> bool {
    var i: u64 = 0
    while i < sched.NETOK_MAX {
        let e: u64 = allow_at(state, t, i)
        if (e & 1) != 0 {
            let ea: u64 = (e >> 32) & 0xFFFFFFFF
            let ep: u64 = (e >> 16) & 0xFFFF
            let m: u64 = mask_of((e >> 8) & 0xFF)
            if ((addr & 0xFFFFFFFF) & m) == (ea & m) {
                if ep == 0 || ep == port {
                    return true
                }
            }
        }
        i = i + 1
    }
    return false
}

// -------------------------------------------------------- THE DOOR
//
// Every outbound socket call in `sys.fi` asks this and nothing else.
// One question, one place, four answers -- the shape `perm.may` gave the
// file rights in round K13, for the same reason.
fn decide(state: u64, t: u64, addr: u64, port: u64) -> u64 {
    let v: u64 = view_of(state, t)
    if v == V_REAL {
        return D_REAL
    }
    if v == V_NONE {
        kstate.add(state, sc(NV_DENIED), 1)
        return D_DENY
    }
    if v == V_FILTERED {
        if allowed(state, t, addr, port) {
            return D_REAL
        }
        return D_FAKE
    }
    return D_FAKE
}

// -------------------------------------------- the faked socket itself

fn is_fake(state: u64, i: u64) -> bool {
    if i >= inet.SOCK_MAX {
        return false
    }
    return kstate.get(state, at(i) + F_USE) != 0
}

// Does this process have any faked socket open at all? What the taskbar
// asks in order to put a mark on the window, and the reason the mark is
// truthful rather than decorative.
fn marked(state: u64, t: u64) -> bool {
    return view_of(state, t) != V_REAL
}

// A socket slot is handed out again. Called from `sys.do_socket` and
// `sys.do_accept` right after `inet.sock_new`, unconditionally -- so a
// slot that some earlier close forgot to clear cannot make a real
// socket of a real process look faked.
fn reset(state: u64, i: u64) {
    if i >= inet.SOCK_MAX {
        return
    }
    let a: u64 = at(i)
    kstate.set(state, a + F_USE, 0)
    kstate.set(state, a + F_KIND, 0)
    kstate.set(state, a + F_ADDR, 0)
    kstate.set(state, a + F_PORT, 0)
    kstate.set(state, a + F_POS, 0)
    kstate.set(state, a + F_LEN, 0)
    kstate.set(state, a + F_SENT, 0)
    kstate.set(state, a + F_SEEN, 0)
}

fn release(state: u64, i: u64) {
    reset(state, i)
}

fn peer_of(state: u64, i: u64, addr: *mut u64, port: *mut u64) {
    *addr = 0
    *port = 0
    if i >= inet.SOCK_MAX {
        return
    }
    *addr = kstate.get(state, at(i) + F_ADDR)
    *port = kstate.get(state, at(i) + F_PORT)
}

// A connection into the invented network. It succeeds in this call, on
// this stack, with no wire and no waiting -- that is the whole promise
// of the mode and the reason `connect` in `faked` costs microseconds.
fn open_fake(state: u64, i: u64, kind: u64, addr: u64, port: u64) -> u64 {
    if i >= inet.SOCK_MAX {
        return errno.neg(errno.E_BADF)
    }
    let a: u64 = at(i)
    kstate.set(state, a + F_USE, 1)
    kstate.set(state, a + F_KIND, kind)
    kstate.set(state, a + F_ADDR, addr)
    kstate.set(state, a + F_PORT, port)
    kstate.set(state, a + F_POS, 0)
    kstate.set(state, a + F_LEN, 0)
    kstate.set(state, a + F_SEEN, 0)
    kstate.add(state, sc(NV_OPENED), 1)
    return 0
}

// ---------------------------------------------------- writing a line
//
// The canned answers are built octet by octet into the socket's own 256
// octets. `\r\n` is written as the two numbers and not as an escape,
// because what an HTTP header ends with is exactly 13 and 10 and a
// reader of this file should be able to see that.

fn put(state: u64, i: u64, off: u64, v: u64) -> u64 {
    if off >= NV_BUFB {
        return off
    }
    kstate.set8(state + buf(i) + off, (v & 0xFF) as u8)
    return off + 1
}

fn put_text(state: u64, i: u64, off: u64, text: u64) -> u64 {
    var o: u64 = off
    var k: u64 = 0
    while kstate.get8(text + k) != 0 {
        o = put(state, i, o, kstate.get8(text + k))
        k = k + 1
    }
    return o
}

fn put_line(state: u64, i: u64, off: u64, text: u64) -> u64 {
    var o: u64 = put_text(state, i, off, text)
    o = put(state, i, o, 13)
    return put(state, i, o, 10)
}

// Does what the program wrote look like an HTTP request? Four methods
// and a space, which is all that is needed to tell an HTTP client from
// anything else -- and being wrong here costs nothing: a program that
// gets a 204 it did not ask for reads it as garbage and closes, which
// is what it would have done with an empty stream anyway.
fn looks_http(src: u64, n: u64) -> bool {
    if n < 5 {
        return false
    }
    let a: u64 = kstate.get8(src)
    let b: u64 = kstate.get8(src + 1)
    let c: u64 = kstate.get8(src + 2)
    let d: u64 = kstate.get8(src + 3)
    if a == 71 && b == 69 && c == 84 && d == 32 { // "GET "
        return true
    }
    if a == 80 && b == 79 && c == 83 && d == 84 { // "POST"
        return true
    }
    if a == 72 && b == 69 && c == 65 && d == 68 { // "HEAD"
        return true
    }
    if a == 80 && b == 85 && c == 84 && d == 32 { // "PUT "
        return true
    }
    return false
}

// `HTTP/1.1 204 No Content`, no body, and the connection closes after
// it. 204 and not 200: a 200 with zero octets is a page that failed to
// load, and a browser or an updater will retry it. 204 MEANS "there is
// nothing here, and that is the answer" -- it is what Android's own
// connectivity check asks for, and every HTTP client in existence
// handles it without complaining.
fn build_http(state: u64, i: u64) {
    var l1: [u8; 25] = "HTTP/1.1 204 No Content\0\0"
    var l2: [u8; 24] = "Server: osum-netview\0\0\0\0"
    var l3: [u8; 20] = "Content-Length: 0\0\0\0"
    var l4: [u8; 20] = "Connection: close\0\0\0"
    var o: u64 = 0
    o = put_line(state, i, o, (&l1[0]) as u64)
    o = put_line(state, i, o, (&l2[0]) as u64)
    o = put_line(state, i, o, (&l3[0]) as u64)
    o = put_line(state, i, o, (&l4[0]) as u64)
    o = put(state, i, o, 13)
    o = put(state, i, o, 10)
    kstate.set(state, at(i) + F_LEN, o)
    kstate.set(state, at(i) + F_POS, 0)
    kstate.add(state, sc(NV_REPLIES), 1)
}

// -------------------------------------------------------------- TCP

// Everything the program writes is taken and thrown away, and the count
// of what was thrown away is kept -- because "no octet left the machine"
// is only worth something next to "and this many were offered".
fn send_fake(state: u64, i: u64, src: u64, n: u64) -> u64 {
    if i >= inet.SOCK_MAX {
        return errno.neg(errno.E_BADF)
    }
    let a: u64 = at(i)
    kstate.set(state, a + F_SENT, kstate.get(state, a + F_SENT) + n)
    kstate.add(state, sc(NV_DROPPED), n)
    if kstate.get(state, a + F_SEEN) == 0 {
        kstate.set(state, a + F_SEEN, 1)
        if looks_http(src, n) {
            build_http(state, i)
        }
    }
    return n
}

// What is left of the canned answer, then an ordinary end of stream.
// NEVER -EAGAIN: a faked socket must not make the caller in `sys.fi`
// sleep, because a mode whose whole purpose is not to hang may not hang.
fn recv_fake(state: u64, i: u64, dst: u64, n: u64) -> u64 {
    if i >= inet.SOCK_MAX {
        return errno.neg(errno.E_BADF)
    }
    let a: u64 = at(i)
    let len: u64 = kstate.get(state, a + F_LEN)
    let pos: u64 = kstate.get(state, a + F_POS)
    if pos >= len {
        return 0
    }
    var take: u64 = len - pos
    if take > n {
        take = n
    }
    var k: u64 = 0
    while k < take {
        kstate.set8(dst + k, kstate.get8(state + buf(i) + pos + k) as u8)
        k = k + 1
    }
    kstate.set(state, a + F_POS, pos + take)
    return take
}

// ------------------------------------------------------ UDP and DNS

// The length of the question section of a DNS message, from octet 12:
// labels until the zero octet, then four for QTYPE and QCLASS. Returns 0
// if the message runs out first -- an answer built on a malformed
// question would be worse than no answer.
fn question_len(src: u64, n: u64) -> u64 {
    var o: u64 = 12
    var going: bool = true
    while going {
        if o >= n {
            return 0
        }
        let l: u64 = kstate.get8(src + o)
        if l == 0 {
            o = o + 1
            going = false
        } else if l >= 64 {
            return 0 // a compression pointer in a question: refuse
        } else {
            o = o + 1 + l
        }
    }
    if o + 4 > n {
        return 0
    }
    return o + 4 - 12
}

// The answer to a name: the same identifier, the same question, and one
// A record pointing at FAKE_SINK. A question that is not an A record is
// answered with NOERROR and no records -- the resolver then stops
// instead of retrying, which is the whole point.
fn build_dns(state: u64, i: u64, src: u64, n: u64) -> bool {
    if n < 17 {
        return false
    }
    let qlen: u64 = question_len(src, n)
    if qlen == 0 || 12 + qlen + 16 > NV_BUFB {
        return false
    }
    let qtype: u64 = (kstate.get8(src + 12 + qlen - 4) << 8)
        | kstate.get8(src + 12 + qlen - 3)
    var o: u64 = 0
    o = put(state, i, o, kstate.get8(src)) // the identifier, unchanged
    o = put(state, i, o, kstate.get8(src + 1))
    // Flags: QR=1, the opcode of the question, AA=0, TC=0,
    // RD as it was asked, RA=1, RCODE=0.
    o = put(state, i, o, 0x80 | (kstate.get8(src + 2) & 0x7A))
    o = put(state, i, o, 0x80)
    o = put(state, i, o, 0) // QDCOUNT = 1
    o = put(state, i, o, 1)
    var answers: u64 = 0
    if qtype == 1 {
        answers = 1
    }
    o = put(state, i, o, 0) // ANCOUNT
    o = put(state, i, o, answers)
    o = put(state, i, o, 0) // NSCOUNT = 0
    o = put(state, i, o, 0)
    o = put(state, i, o, 0) // ARCOUNT = 0
    o = put(state, i, o, 0)
    var k: u64 = 0
    while k < qlen {
        o = put(state, i, o, kstate.get8(src + 12 + k))
        k = k + 1
    }
    if answers == 1 {
        o = put(state, i, o, 0xC0) // a pointer back to octet 12
        o = put(state, i, o, 0x0C)
        o = put(state, i, o, 0) // TYPE A
        o = put(state, i, o, 1)
        o = put(state, i, o, 0) // CLASS IN
        o = put(state, i, o, 1)
        o = put(state, i, o, 0) // TTL 60 seconds
        o = put(state, i, o, 0)
        o = put(state, i, o, 0)
        o = put(state, i, o, 60)
        o = put(state, i, o, 0) // RDLENGTH 4
        o = put(state, i, o, 4)
        o = put(state, i, o, (FAKE_SINK >> 24) & 0xFF)
        o = put(state, i, o, (FAKE_SINK >> 16) & 0xFF)
        o = put(state, i, o, (FAKE_SINK >> 8) & 0xFF)
        o = put(state, i, o, FAKE_SINK & 0xFF)
    }
    kstate.set(state, at(i) + F_LEN, o)
    kstate.set(state, at(i) + F_POS, 0)
    kstate.add(state, sc(NV_REPLIES), 1)
    return true
}

// An echo request turns into an echo reply. The kernel's ping socket
// hands the whole ICMP message down (`kernel/user/ping.fi` builds it,
// `inet.icmp_send` fills the checksum), so the reply is that message
// with the type changed from 8 to 0. `ping` in a faked process therefore
// answers itself, in microseconds, and says so.
fn build_icmp(state: u64, i: u64, src: u64, n: u64) -> bool {
    if n < 8 || n > NV_BUFB {
        return false
    }
    var o: u64 = 0
    o = put(state, i, o, 0) // ICMP echo REPLY
    var k: u64 = 1
    while k < n {
        o = put(state, i, o, kstate.get8(src + k))
        k = k + 1
    }
    kstate.set(state, at(i) + F_LEN, o)
    kstate.set(state, at(i) + F_POS, 0)
    kstate.add(state, sc(NV_REPLIES), 1)
    return true
}

fn sendto_fake(state: u64, i: u64, src: u64, n: u64, addr: u64,
    port: u64) -> u64 {
    if i >= inet.SOCK_MAX {
        return errno.neg(errno.E_BADF)
    }
    let a: u64 = at(i)
    let kind: u64 = kstate.get(state, a + F_KIND)
    kstate.set(state, a + F_SENT, kstate.get(state, a + F_SENT) + n)
    kstate.add(state, sc(NV_DROPPED), n)
    kstate.set(state, a + F_ADDR, addr)
    kstate.set(state, a + F_PORT, port)
    if kind == inet.SK_ICMP {
        build_icmp(state, i, src, n)
        return n
    }
    if kind == inet.SK_TCP {
        return send_fake(state, i, src, n)
    }
    if port == 53 {
        build_dns(state, i, src, n)
    }
    return n
}

// A datagram is not a stream: what is there comes out in ONE call, and
// what is not there is -EAGAIN and not an end of stream, because a
// datagram socket has no end. -EAGAIN comes back AT ONCE -- `sys.fi`
// does not put a faked socket into its waiting loop.
fn recvfrom_fake(state: u64, i: u64, dst: u64, n: u64, addr: *mut u64,
    port: *mut u64) -> u64 {
    *addr = 0
    *port = 0
    if i >= inet.SOCK_MAX {
        return errno.neg(errno.E_BADF)
    }
    let a: u64 = at(i)
    if kstate.get(state, a + F_KIND) == inet.SK_TCP {
        return recv_fake(state, i, dst, n)
    }
    let len: u64 = kstate.get(state, a + F_LEN)
    let pos: u64 = kstate.get(state, a + F_POS)
    if pos >= len {
        return errno.neg(errno.E_AGAIN)
    }
    var take: u64 = len - pos
    if take > n {
        take = n
    }
    var k: u64 = 0
    while k < take {
        kstate.set8(dst + k, kstate.get8(state + buf(i) + pos + k) as u8)
        k = k + 1
    }
    kstate.set(state, a + F_POS, len)
    *addr = kstate.get(state, a + F_ADDR)
    *port = kstate.get(state, a + F_PORT)
    return take
}

// ----------------------------------------------------- inheritance
//
// A child gets the view of its parent, and its allow list with it.
// `execve` needs no line here at all: it keeps the same task record, so
// the view survives the new image by itself -- which is the behaviour
// that matters, because `netview faked <program>` IS a `fork`, a
// tightening and an `execve`.
fn inherit(state: u64, parent: u64, child: u64) {
    sched.tset(state, child, sched.T_NETV,
        sched.tget(state, parent, sched.T_NETV))
    var i: u64 = 0
    while i < sched.NETOK_MAX {
        sched.tset(state, child, sched.T_NETOK + i * 8,
            sched.tget(state, parent, sched.T_NETOK + i * 8))
        i = i + 1
    }
}

// ------------------------------------------------------- the report
//
// Four numbers at the end of a run, in the `key value` shape every other
// measured file of this repository uses. Next to `nic: tx_o=` from
// `netsvc.report` they are the whole acceptance of this round: octets
// offered by faked programs, and octets that left the machine.
fn report(state: u64) {
    var s_drop: [u8; 15] = "nv: dropped=\0\0\0"
    var s_open: [u8; 14] = "nv: opened=\0\0\0"
    var s_deny: [u8; 14] = "nv: denied=\0\0\0"
    var s_rep: [u8; 15] = "nv: replies=\0\0\0"
    var s_wire: [u8; 15] = "nv: wire_o=\0\0\0\0"
    serial.puts((&s_drop[0]) as u64)
    serial.dec(dropped(state))
    serial.nl()
    serial.puts((&s_open[0]) as u64)
    serial.dec(opened(state))
    serial.nl()
    serial.puts((&s_deny[0]) as u64)
    serial.dec(denials(state))
    serial.nl()
    serial.puts((&s_rep[0]) as u64)
    serial.dec(replies(state))
    serial.nl()
    // DIE ZAHL DER RUNDE, und sie kommt nicht aus dieser Datei: es ist
    // der Oktettzaehler des KARTENTREIBERS, gefuehrt in
    // `virtio.tx_frame`, wo jeder Rahmen dieses Kernels durchmuss. Ein
    // Lauf, in dem nur vorgetaeuschte Prozesse gearbeitet haben, muss
    // hier 0 stehen haben -- und das ist eine Messung an einer Stelle,
    // die von dieser Runde nichts weiss.
    serial.puts((&s_wire[0]) as u64)
    serial.dec(virtio.tx_octets(state))
    serial.nl()
}
